 ## Background
-The Discrete Inverse Radon Transform is the mathematical foundation of computed tomography (CT) reconstruction. It is derived from the Fourier Slice Theorem, which states that the 1D Fourier transform of a projection at angle $\theta$ is a slice of the 2D Fourier transform of the image at that angle [S1, unofficial]. The method was formalized in the context of computerized tomography in the 1970s, with seminal work by Kak and Slaney [S1, unofficial]. It is widely used in medical imaging, industrial CT, and light field synthesis [S2].
